======================================================================
(2) NESTED concentric ('diameter onion')  vs  endpoint-anchored chain
======================================================================
  n=5: concentric [(1, 5), (2, 4)] -> H=15;  anchored [(1, 5), (1, 3)] -> H=9, regular(rotational) H=15
  n=6: concentric [(1, 6), (2, 5)] -> H=37;  anchored [(1, 6), (1, 4)] -> H=19
  n=7: concentric [(1, 7), (2, 6), (3, 5)] -> H=123;  anchored [(1, 7), (1, 5), (1, 3)] -> H=31, regular(rotational) H=175
  n=8: concentric [(1, 8), (2, 7), (3, 6)] -> H=389;  anchored [(1, 8), (1, 6), (1, 4)] -> H=73
  n=9: concentric [(1, 9), (2, 8), (3, 7), (4, 6)] -> H=1479;  anchored [(1, 9), (1, 7), (1, 5), (1, 3)] -> H=117, regular(rotational) H=3267

  Reading: concentric 'diameter onion' apex flips climb toward the regular
  tournament H (the LRC tight witness); endpoint-anchored flips do not. The
  apex recursion is COUPLED (S520o): H is not multiplicative even over a
  separating vertex -- the recursive sub-ranking dependence is real.
